CAMPUSCART
Full-Stack Development Prompts
Separate prompts for Frontend, Backend, Database, and Final Integration
Project overview
CampusCart is a student-to-student campus marketplace for listing and discovering permitted second-hand items such as textbooks, calculators, stationery, and lab equipment. This document contains coordinated prompts intended for a coding AI. Run them in order and keep the generated files in one project.
Recommended stack: React + Vite + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS; Node.js + Express + TypeScript; MySQL + Prisma ORM; Zod validation; JWT-based sessions in secure HTTP-only cookies.
 
Prompt 1 — Frontend
React + Vite + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS
Copy this prompt into your coding AI to generate the user interface and API integration.
You are a senior frontend engineer. Build the complete frontend for CampusCart, a student-to-student campus marketplace.
STACK: React, Vite, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, React Router, TanStack Query, React Hook Form, Zod, and Lucide React. Use npm.
DESIGN: Create a modern, responsive light theme with white backgrounds, subtle gray borders, an indigo accent, consistent spacing, accessible contrast, reusable components, polished product cards, skeleton loaders, empty states, and toast notifications. Support mobile, tablet, and desktop.
PAGES: Home/product feed, registration, login, product details, create listing, edit listing, my listings, wishlist, messages, profile, admin dashboard, 404, and unauthorized pages.
FEATURES: Product search, category and price filters, pagination, image previews, form validation, wishlist toggling, listing status badges, seller messaging/contact requests, report listing, mark as sold, profile editing, and admin moderation. Show controls appropriate to the authenticated user's role. Never fake authentication or pretend requests succeeded.
API CONTRACT: Read the API base URL from VITE_API_BASE_URL, defaulting to http://localhost:4000/api/v1. Send credentials: "include" for API requests. Use these routes:
- POST /auth/register
- POST /auth/login
- POST /auth/logout
- GET /auth/me
- GET /products and GET /products/:id
- POST /products, PATCH /products/:id, DELETE /products/:id
- GET /users/me/listings
- GET /users/me/wishlist
- POST /users/me/wishlist/:productId
- DELETE /users/me/wishlist/:productId
- GET /users/me/profile and PATCH /users/me/profile
- GET /conversations and POST /conversations
- GET /conversations/:id/messages and POST /conversations/:id/messages
- POST /reports
- GET /admin/reports
- PATCH /admin/reports/:id
- PATCH /admin/products/:id/status
Use the backend's documented response envelope and exact field names. Do not invent alternate endpoints.
SECURITY: Treat frontend validation as a convenience, not a security boundary. Never store JWTs in localStorage. Do not expose secrets in VITE variables. Handle 401, 403, 404, 409, 422, and 500 responses. Rely on backend authorization for actual enforcement.
IMPLEMENTATION: Build a working frontend, not a static mockup. Create a reusable API client, typed models, auth state, protected routes, role-aware navigation, loading/error states, and functional forms. Include .env.example, a clear folder structure, complete source code, installation commands, and a README. Do not leave TODOs, pseudocode, missing imports, or nonfunctional buttons. Run the build and fix TypeScript errors before finishing.
 
Prompt 2 — Backend
Node.js + Express + TypeScript + Prisma
Use this prompt to generate the REST API. Keep the routes and contract aligned with the frontend.
You are a senior backend engineer. Implement the REST API for CampusCart, a student campus marketplace.
STACK: Node.js, Express, TypeScript, Prisma ORM, MySQL, Zod validation, Argon2id password hashing, JWT authentication in secure HTTP-only cookies, Helmet, CORS, rate limiting, and structured logging. Use npm and strict TypeScript.
BASE URL: /api/v1. Default development port: 4000.
ROUTES:
- POST /auth/register, POST /auth/login, POST /auth/logout, GET /auth/me
- GET /products, GET /products/:id, POST /products, PATCH /products/:id, DELETE /products/:id
- GET /users/me/listings
- GET /users/me/wishlist, POST /users/me/wishlist/:productId, DELETE /users/me/wishlist/:productId
- GET /users/me/profile, PATCH /users/me/profile
- GET /conversations, POST /conversations
- GET /conversations/:id/messages, POST /conversations/:id/messages
- POST /reports
- GET /admin/reports, PATCH /admin/reports/:id, PATCH /admin/products/:id/status
DATA CONTRACT: Follow the shared Prisma schema. Roles: STUDENT and ADMIN. Product conditions: NEW, LIKE_NEW, GOOD, FAIR. Product statuses: AVAILABLE, RESERVED, SOLD, REMOVED. Use consistent camelCase JSON fields, ISO-8601 timestamps, and documented pagination. Product filters: search, category, minPrice, maxPrice, condition, page, limit. Use a consistent success envelope such as { data, meta? } and error envelope { error: { code, message, details? } }.
FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS: Implement registration, login, logout, current-user lookup, product CRUD, listing ownership checks, image upload validation, search and pagination, wishlist, profile, conversations, messages, reports, and admin moderation. Use database transactions for related changes that must be atomic. Prevent duplicate conversations for the same participants and product where appropriate. Validate every request body, path parameter, and query parameter.
SECURITY: Hash passwords with Argon2id. Implement a clearly documented signed session/JWT design with short-lived access tokens where appropriate. Store browser authentication tokens only in HTTP-only cookies; configure Secure in production, SameSite appropriately, cookie expiry, origin allowlisting, and CSRF protection for state-changing cookie-authenticated requests. Rate-limit authentication and abuse-prone endpoints. Enforce ownership and ADMIN authorization on the server. Never trust client-supplied role, seller ID, price, or ownership claims. Validate upload type, size, and file content; generate safe filenames. Use Prisma parameterized operations. Never log passwords, cookies, or tokens.
CONFIGURATION: Read DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET, CLIENT_ORIGIN, PORT, NODE_ENV, and storage settings from environment variables. Fail startup clearly if required production configuration is missing. Include .env.example with placeholders only.
ERROR HANDLING: Add centralized error middleware, 404 handling, validation errors, appropriate HTTP status codes, request logging, and safe error messages. Configure CORS for the frontend origin with credentials enabled; do not use wildcard origins with credentials.
QUALITY: Organize code into routes, controllers, services, middleware, validators, types, and database access. Provide a health endpoint, API documentation, seed script, unit/integration tests, and setup instructions. Test unauthorized access, invalid input, duplicate records, and ownership violations. Generate runnable source files, not TODOs or fake responses. Run tests and TypeScript compilation and fix failures before finishing.
 
Prompt 3 — Database
MySQL + Prisma ORM
Use this prompt to generate the relational schema, migrations, indexes, and development seed data.
You are a senior database engineer. Design and implement the MySQL database for CampusCart, a student-to-student campus marketplace. Use Prisma ORM and TypeScript.
DELIVERABLES: prisma/schema.prisma, migration files, seed script, database indexes, relational constraints, an ER diagram in Mermaid, and setup documentation. Use InnoDB, utf8mb4, explicit relations, suitable decimal precision for money, and UTC timestamps. Select a compatible stable Prisma version and ensure the schema validates.
MODELS:
1. User: id, name, unique normalized email, passwordHash, role (STUDENT or ADMIN), optional collegeName, optional avatarUrl, createdAt, updatedAt.
2. Category: id, unique name, slug, createdAt.
3. Product: id, sellerId referencing User, categoryId referencing Category, title, description, price using Decimal with suitable precision, condition (NEW, LIKE_NEW, GOOD, FAIR), status (AVAILABLE, RESERVED, SOLD, REMOVED), createdAt, updatedAt.
4. ProductImage: id, productId, imageUrl or storage key, position, createdAt. Store references to images, not image binaries.
5. WishlistItem: id, userId, productId, createdAt; unique constraint on userId + productId.
6. Conversation: id, participantOneId, participantTwoId, productId, createdAt, updatedAt. Both participants reference User. Define a canonical participant order and a tested strategy to prevent duplicate conversations for the same participant pair and product.
7. Message: id, conversationId, senderId, body, createdAt, optional readAt. Ensure application logic verifies that the sender is a participant.
8. Report: id, reporterId, optional productId, optional reportedUserId, reason, optional description, status (OPEN, REVIEWING, RESOLVED, DISMISSED), createdAt, updatedAt, optional reviewedById. Require a valid report target at the application layer.
RELATIONSHIPS: Users own products and messages. Products belong to a seller and category and have images. WishlistItem joins User and Product. Conversations have two participants, optional product context, and many messages. Reports reference reporter and optional targets, plus an optional reviewing admin. Ensure every Prisma relation compiles.
INTEGRITY: Add primary keys, foreign keys, unique constraints, and indexes for product status/category/price/createdAt, sellerId, wishlist lookups, conversation participants, message pagination, and report moderation. Prevent duplicate wishlist items and duplicate conversations. Define onDelete behavior explicitly. Prefer restricting deletion of referenced users/products or soft removal when history must be preserved; do not accidentally cascade-delete reports or message history.
SEARCH: Support case-insensitive title search and filters for category, minPrice, maxPrice, condition, and status. Document MySQL collation choices and implement filters with safe Prisma query construction. Do not claim full-text search unless configured.
SEED DATA: Add repeatable development-only categories such as Textbooks, Electronics, Lab Equipment, and Stationery, plus fictional student accounts and sample listings. Never use real personal data or production passwords. Document test credentials and prevent seed execution against production.
CONFIGURATION: Read DATABASE_URL from environment variables. Include a placeholder-only example URL, commands for generating Prisma Client, applying development migrations, seeding, and deploying migrations safely. Do not automatically drop or reset an existing database.
QUALITY CHECKS: Validate relations and enum names against generated Prisma Client. Ensure monetary fields, nullable values, uniqueness, and indexes match API expectations. Add integration tests for foreign keys, duplicate wishlists, duplicate conversations, report relations, and pagination ordering. Run Prisma validation and migration checks. Output complete runnable files, not just SQL snippets or explanations.
 
Prompt 4 — Integration audit
Full-stack consistency and testing
Run this only after the frontend, backend, and database files exist.
Act as a senior full-stack engineer auditing the actual CampusCart frontend, backend, and database files. Do not assume a feature exists without inspecting its implementation.
1. Compare all frontend API calls against actual backend routes.
2. Compare request bodies, response types, enum values, nullable fields, and pagination formats.
3. Compare Prisma models, migrations, and backend queries.
4. Verify cookie authentication, CORS, CSRF protections, role authorization, and ownership checks.
5. Test registration, login, logout, product creation, image uploads, search, filters, wishlist, messaging, reports, and admin moderation.
6. Check broken imports, TypeScript errors, missing environment variables, invalid Prisma relations, and missing migrations.
7. Fix confirmed defects in the actual files. Do not replace working implementations with mock data.
8. Add or update integration tests for critical workflows and unauthorized access.
9. Run the build, Prisma validation, and available test suites. Report actual results, including failures that remain.
Do not claim the project is complete or production-ready unless the corresponding checks have actually passed. Preserve existing data and never reset a production database.
 
Recommended build order
1.	Agree on the shared data contract and field names.
2.	Create and validate the Prisma schema; run development migrations.
3.	Implement the backend and test its routes.
4.	Build the frontend and connect it to the real API.
5.	Run the integration audit and fix issues before deployment.
Important notes
•	Keep secrets in local environment files and never commit them to source control.
•	Never store authentication tokens in localStorage when using the cookie-based design specified above.
•	Frontend validation improves usability; backend validation and authorization enforce security.
•	Use fictional seed data only, and never run destructive database reset commands against production.
•	Generated code must be executed and tested. A prompt alone cannot guarantee a bug-free application.
